        <p>The freelancer keeps $5,736.99 less a year on the same gross, or $478.08 less a month. The reason is in the middle of the table: the freelancer’s federal income tax is actually lower, $14,985.21 against $16,795.60, because half the self-employment tax is deductible and pulls taxable income down to $92,150.95. But the self-employment tax itself is $16,458.10, against $8,910.72 of FICA for the employee, and that $7,547.38 difference swallows the federal saving twice over. Self-employment tax is 15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings, which is $107,569.28 here, and it is due every quarter, not withheld from a paycheck, so the freelancer also has to hold $1,371.51 a month back for it. Two caveats before you quote the gap. These figures are before the 20% qualified business income deduction, which can lower a freelancer’s federal tax, and before business expenses, which lower the self-employment tax base. Both shrink the gap, and neither is priced here. The rate that closes the gap is the number to carry into a negotiation. To keep the same $90,773.68 as an employee, a freelancer billing 2,080 hours a year needs about $60.22 an hour, on gross of about $125,248.84. Bill only 1,800 hours, with the rest lost to finding work and admin, and the rate has to be about $69.58 an hour. Bill 1,500 hours and it is about $83.50 an hour. That is why freelancers quote a higher rate than employees earn: the rate has to cover the tax the employer used to pay, plus the hours nobody bills for. Our <a href="/articles/hourly-vs-salary-which-pays-more/">hourly-versus-salary comparison</a> prices the employee side of the same choice, and our <a href="/calculators/paycheck/">paycheck calculator</a> runs the employee math on any rate and schedule.</p>
