        <p>Two rows cause the mix-ups. Biweekly and semi-monthly sound alike and pay differently: biweekly is 26 paychecks of $4,480.00, semi-monthly is 24 paychecks of $4,853.33, and the two extra biweekly checks a year are what close the gap between them. A month with three biweekly checks pays $13,440.00 gross ($10,473.90 take-home), while a normal two-check month pays $8,960.00 ($6,982.60 take-home), so biweekly pay jumps around from month to month. The monthly row has its own trap: it divides by 12, not by 4. Four weeks is only $8,960.00, and the $746.67 difference is the extra 1.33 weeks the average month actually holds. Our <a href="/articles/how-many-paychecks-in-a-year/">paychecks-per-year guide</a> maps the three-paycheck months for every 2026 cycle, and our <a href="/articles/biweekly-vs-semi-monthly-pay-difference/">biweekly-versus-semimonthly comparison</a> runs the two schedules through a full year on the same salary.</p>
