        <p>Read the last column top to bottom and the pattern is plain: fewer hours, more kept from each one. At 20 hours a week you keep $47.09 an hour. At 40 hours you keep $43.64. That is a $3.45-an-hour gap on the same $56.00 rate, and the bracket is the reason. A 20-hour year has $42,140.00 of taxable income, and none of it touches the 22% bracket. A 25-hour year has $56,700.00, and $6,300.00 of it lands in the 22% bracket, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket shows up at all. Three rows deserve a second look. First, the 35-hour week crosses $100,000 gross on its own, at $101,920.00, and its $80,530.72 of take-home already beats a $100,000 salary by $1,350.72, before overtime or a raise. Second, the 37.5-hour paid week, the 7.5-hour office day, pays $109,200.00 gross, which still beats a $105,000 salary by $4,200.00 gross and $2,954.70 of take-home. Give back 2.5 hours a week and you give up $7,280.00 gross and $5,121.48 of take-home. Third, going from 30 to 40 hours is worth $29,120.00 gross and $20,485.92 of take-home ($1,707.16 a month), the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Part-time keeps the bigger share too: at 20 hours you keep 84.1% of gross, against 77.9% full-time, and a 25-hour week at $56 beats a $70,000 salary’s take-home by $1,969.80, while a 20-hour week beats a full-time $27-an-hour job by $1,671.28. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> runs the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
