      <section class="article-section" id="limitations">
        <h2>Limitations</h2>
        <p>These are the limits of the numbers on this page, and knowing them keeps the figures honest. The take-home sketch is federal only unless stated, using 2026 federal rules for a single filer taking the $16,100 standard deduction. State and local taxes are excluded, so your actual take-home is lower in most states. The $116,480.00 headline assumes 40 paid hours in all 52 weeks. Part-time hours, unpaid weeks, and seasonal gaps lower it, and the schedules and unpaid-weeks tables price exactly how much. Overtime is not guaranteed: the overtime sections assume those extra hours are actually there and paid at time and a half. Exempt workers get no overtime premium, and some employers cap overtime hours. The 22% bracket slice is exact for this rate only. The $49,980.00 figure depends on $116,480.00 of gross and the $16,100 standard deduction, and a 401(k) contribution, a different filing status, or a second income changes where the bracket line falls. The 24% bracket starts at $105,700.00 of taxable income, or about $58.56 an hour on straight time, though five overtime hours a week already reach it at $56 an hour. Benefits are priced separately: the salary comparisons exclude health premiums, 401(k) matches, and paid time off unless we say otherwise in a note, and a strong benefits package can flip the $115,000 row. Rounding matters at the edges: per-check figures are rounded to the cent, so multiplying a rounded monthly figure by 12 can differ from the annual figure by a few cents. And this article is general information, not tax or legal advice. These figures are 2026 federal estimates for a single filer taking the standard deduction, using the same method as the site’s other guides, and your employer’s payroll calendar, withholding elections, and benefit deductions change the figures.</p>
      </section>

